{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>For the people come to me, etc.</b> It is difficult: what kind of answer is this? For Jethro saw that the people came to him to inquire, and the intent of his question was why he conducts himself in this practice, since there is a burden upon the people. And his words are clear from his saying ״you sit alone,״ meaning, and there are no other judges to judge the people. And even if Moses had not understood his words, he should have asked the force of the statement, rather than saying things that he himself was questioning." "It appears that Moses understood from Jethro's words, in his saying <b>that you do</b> etc., that Moses was constraining them and obligating them not to stand before any judge other than himself, and this is the reason the people endure the burden; and if he wished, he could appoint judges over them and they would go to be judged before them. To this he answered him that it was not from him but from the people, for they come of their own accord to me and do not desire to go to anyone else. And regarding what should be difficult for you — why they would desire this burden — the reason is <b>because they have a matter that comes to me</b>, and they of blessed memory said (Zohar vol. II 78a) that the matter itself comes to me and the judgment is justified in truth, and this thing is not found in anyone but me." "<b>And now</b> there is no basis to appoint judges and have the people go before them, and he finished speaking <b>and I make known</b> etc., meaning, this thing too is impossible without me." "And Jethro was in doubt about the meaning of Moses' words, which bear two possibilities. The first: since the people come to him, he is zealous for the mitzvah to judge between a man and his fellow etc., to perform it himself and not others. The second: that the people would not want anyone but him, for the reason he explained in his words. To this he answered, against what is understood from his words — that he chooses to perform the mitzvah himself and would not refrain from doing good — he said <b>the thing is not good that you do</b> etc., as he will explain the reason. And against what is understood from his words — that the people would not be willing to go to anyone else — he said <b>I will advise you</b> etc. <b>you be for the people</b> etc. <b>and you shall see</b> etc. <b>and they shall judge the people at all times</b>, meaning, in every case where the ruling is acceptable to both litigants; and presumably, when the judges rule for them an acceptable ruling, why would they choose the great burden? <b>and it shall be</b> etc., meaning, and when there is a great matter involving the intensification of the judgment — whether on account of the judges, that they cannot grasp it, or on account of the litigants, that the two parties are not satisfied with their ruling — they shall bring to you, and it is not on account of this particular matter that you are obligated in everything."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:29:44.604970864Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}